        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "We factor $6{x}^{2}-360x+4\\,800$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(6{x}^{2},360x,4\\,800)=6\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-360x+4\\,800 & = \\\\ 6\\cdot ({x}^{2})+6\\cdot (-60x)+6\\cdot (800)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot ({x}^{2})+6\\cdot (-60x)+6\\cdot (800) & = \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-60x+800)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-60x+800.$",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245623/observations/step-6-80c952d18a9ba7031648.html"
        }
